<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
	Static analysis over src/, this extension's hand-written PHP. An extension
	with none (a theme, a datamodel-only package) skips the job entirely.

	CodeQL has no PHP support and phpcs (phpcs.xml.dist) is a coding-standard
	linter, not a security scanner - but what runs here is Psalm's plain
	type-correctness analysis, not a security check either. The
	vulnerability-class scanning is the progpilot job in ci.yml; see that
	job's comment for why it was chosen over Psalm's taint analysis and the
	other scanners measured.

	The taint-analysis flag is off. In altioo-mcp, where this configuration
	comes from, it was measured vacuous rather than slow: its input arrived as
	a PSR-7 object built inside vendor/, which this config never scans, and
	its sinks (MetaModel, DBSearch, UserRights) are the undefined symbols
	suppressed below, so the analyser had no source and no sink to connect. An
	extension whose src/ reads input itself may find otherwise - measure it
	against a positive control ($_GET fed straight to echo is flagged
	TaintedHtml) before deciding either way.

	This job runs with no iTop checkout, so every core symbol an extension
	calls (MetaModel, DBObject, UserRights, cmdbAbstractObject, and the rest)
	is genuinely undefined to Psalm here, not merely unresolved. The
	UndefinedClass family below is suppressed for exactly that reason and no
	other: "undefined" is not a finding when the definition was never going
	to be in this tree. Nothing under vendor/ is scanned either, per
	itop-extension-guide.md §7.1.

	findUnusedCode is off for the same class of reason: this scan sees only
	src/, never iTop calling an extension point by convention or through
	InterfaceDiscovery, and never another extension calling a public API
	from outside this repository. Every one of those would read as unused
	code that plainly is not.

	psalm-baseline.xml ships empty: no finding is accepted until somebody
	accepts it. Psalm refuses to run when the file named below is missing,
	so keep it even while it lists nothing. To accept the findings an
	existing src/ already has, regenerate it with Psalm's set-baseline flag
	and review what went in. findUnusedBaselineEntry is off because a
	baseline entry matches by file, issue type and the exact reported code
	snippet, so an unrelated reformat a line above is enough to make one
	stop matching; turning it on would fail the build on that drift rather
	than on anything new.
-->
<psalm
	errorLevel="4"
	resolveFromConfigFile="true"
	errorBaseline="psalm-baseline.xml"
	findUnusedBaselineEntry="false"
	findUnusedCode="false"
>
	<projectFiles>
		<directory name="src" />
	</projectFiles>

	<issueHandlers>
		<UndefinedClass errorLevel="suppress" />
		<UndefinedDocblockClass errorLevel="suppress" />
		<UndefinedMethod errorLevel="suppress" />
		<UndefinedInterfaceMethod errorLevel="suppress" />
		<UndefinedPropertyFetch errorLevel="suppress" />
		<UndefinedFunction errorLevel="suppress" />
		<UndefinedConstant errorLevel="suppress" />
		<MissingDependency errorLevel="suppress" />
	</issueHandlers>
</psalm>
